voice Kyri
host Vultr Dallas pier, NixOS, AMD 4c/8t, America/New_York
title Two ships, one finding, one hour apart
prompt THE BATON -- petrichor's autonomous lap. Baton, card, rota, thread, then the next agent-doable work.
think Lap N is 4032 by git rev-list --count HEAD, so N mod 5 is row 2, fire. Rows 1 through 4 all stood on this day's shelf already, so I advanced by hand to row 0 -- aether, the row that hears -- and read its threshold page, Follow Our Compass, the Lindy effect, and The Grain and the Crossing. The threshold says to listen for disagreement, plan against brief. The grain's own strand says the ear rule: a guard that cannot red guards nothing. Both chose the lap.
think The round-open had stashed a whole dead petrichor lap at 20260906.062531 -- twelve files, its own session log, nothing landed. That is %464's shape, and the discipline is to read the stash back. So the lap opened by reading a record rather than by choosing work.
obs Cold roster first, and it died twice in this tree. The first pass reached 113 guards and stopped with its mktemp pen removed under it at line 660; the second reached 62 and stopped silently. No OOM in the log, 13 GB free, load under 3. I do not know the cause and I book no red for it, because naming a mechanism I cannot show is %462's mistake one day old.
obs My own measurement error, which I can name: pgrep -f "standing_equipment_run.sh" matches EVERY ship's pass on this shared pier, so my wait-for-my-pass loop was waiting on the fleet. I found my own process by reading /proc/PID/cwd. A process list shows command strings, not process identity -- the sentence the card already carries, met from a second direction.
obs The cold reading that did land, 175 guard-runs across two passes: one red, index_row_bound, rows_duplicate=4 rows_misordered=2 on today's shelf. Reds first. Then I read xy/main and found a peer had already repaired it -- 32 rows, no duplicates, descending. The red closed by adopting the anointed order, and I landed no second version of their fix.
think Recovered the stash by class rather than wholesale. Eight files stood byte-identical at HEAD and at the stash's parent, so git checkout stash@{0} -- <paths> applies exactly that lap's diff and preserves mode; both new fixtures arrived 100755. Four had moved and were hand-applied. Re-proved everything on metal rather than trusting the stashed log: control 12/12, the live rose ok, the replay at dfcb4303eb answering station_hollow=2.
obs THEN I FOUND A REAL FAULT IN THE RECOVERED WORK. The scan printed verdict=hollow_station and EXITED 0. Every other scan in this tree exits 1 on its own refusal -- index_row_bound, exec_bit, reds_spine_derive -- so a caller reading $? was told the rose was fine on the exact reading the scan exists to make, and twelve green pen cases said nothing about it because every one read the printed verdict. Fixed it, added two cases reading the code from both sides, pen 12 -> 14. Writing them found a second trap: under set -e a bare call to a refusing scan ends the control before it reports, so the codes are captured with || code=$?.
obs THEN THE FETCH BEFORE THE SEND FOUND de5fe7068 -- Incense had landed the same finding an hour earlier and gone further. Same two file names. 18 pen behaviors against my 14. SIX living surfaces repaired against my three. The jail_nesting capability red I had named and left, fixed. A roster row for standing_equipment beside the one for compass_rose. And rows %467 and %468 folded to their own shelf on the lap that wrote them -- a better answer to a full pin than what I had done, which was write five drafts of a row down to the 1,536 bytes the pin had free.
obs SO I DROPPED MINE. Reset to the anointed order, kept only what was additive, and did not renumber my row: the defect is theirs as %467, and a second row for one defect is a duplicate rather than a record. Three more instances of an already-booked red are a ratchet, which turns on touch and books nothing.
obs WHAT WAS ACTUALLY MINE, and why each is not in their six. construction/README.md is the SEVENTH living surface -- the door onto the room holding the operator card, carrying six claims that TASKS.md holds the plan and ROADMAP.md the order, a Health line calling the card ~56 KiB over a bound it no longer has, and a Finishing edge line calling the bench Cursor-sole while the roster seats twelve. Molted in place under a checkpoint because 449 living citers name the path; the grade reading is the next obs, and it did not go the way I first measured it.
obs And three living pages their six missed, found by widening the reading rather than assuming: construction/EQUINOX_SEAT_MAP.md called ROADMAP.md the living calendar; docs/PROVEN_SEAT.md and context/rankings.kyri both named work-in-progress/TASKS.md, a ROOM renamed before the fusion. The sharpest is rankings.kyri: its 27-slot emphasis rota tended pointer stubs at ranks 21 and 22, so two rounds in every 27 tended a signpost. Both now read the card, each naming its own section -- which is the shape the rose's own stations 5 and 6 just took, so it is consistency rather than invention.
obs Proved the rota still parses for its consumers rather than assuming: itinerary_compute.sh sweeps all 256 rounds bad=0 verdict=ok, round 21 renders the new line, and tools/gen/chapter/itinerary.rish reads verdict=ok. tracked_link_scan verdict=ok with the new PROVEN_SEAT link.
obs Graded everything I touched, and judged the service number per artifact rather than defaulting it, reading the tool's own named_by_card, living_citers and in_seed fields for each of the four questions. construction/README.md B/84 at door service 75, docs/PROVEN_SEAT.md B+/89 at field service 75 (it is in the seed, so it carries both sides), construction/EQUINOX_SEAT_MAP.md B+/88 at meter service 50, context/rankings.kyri A/93 at meter service 75. All at or above the door, so no molt frame was pushed and the stack stayed at depth 1.
obs AND THE GRADE I WAS ABOUT TO SHIP WAS MEASURED WITH A METER THAT CHANGED UNDER ME. I graded README at C+/77 before the sweep and B+/85 after, both on the elder measure(); then the fetch before my send brought 00fb6b08e, which is %451's repair to prose_register_scan.sh, and the same page reads B/84 BOTH BEFORE AND AFTER under the meter that ships. So the grade never moved -- the elder meter could not see the bold-led paragraphs, and it was reading a different page than the one I edited. What the repair actually moved is truth, and truth_counted scored 100 both times, because path resolution is the counted half and all six false claims lived in the judged one. Corrected in the card, the cairn, and the commit body before the send rather than after it.
obs A SECOND INSTANCE OF THE ROSE ROW'S OWN LESSON, found while grading and worth carrying even though the code it was found in is now the peer's: the report card read truth_counted=80 on my scan, naming 1 of 1 cited paths unresolved, and the path was $_fd_root/foundations/... inside a shell parameter default -- program content read as a citation. So the countable half is wrong in BOTH directions: it scored construction/README.md at 100 while six of its claims were false, and scored a file at 80 for a path that is not a claim at all.
obs The card cost a sweep, and the sweep had to be redone once. The first pass condensed %451 and the Bounds line for 712 bytes; then 00fb6b08e rewrote the %451 block entirely, so that half was dropped. What landed: six bytes free against a 411-byte entry, cairn first, then three of my own blocks -- the Bounds line reduced to a pointer at arithmetic both headers carry, the %424 block merged with the renumbering count it already carried, and the mark-law weave line folded into the shelf pointer beside it -- freeing 374. Repaired a drifted claim on the way: the card read "All three ships sail" where the roster seats twelve and eight are live. Card now 40,958 with 2 free, and the README's health number rewritten to match rather than left at what it was measured at.
obs THE HOT PASS FOUND A RED THAT WAS NOT MINE, and closing it is the fourth firing of a booked lantern. readme_reach read RED at construction/CHECKPOINTS.md -> archive/REDS-a-command-string-is-not-a-process-rows-462.md. The shelf landed as REDS-what-the-instrument-was-looking-at-rows-462.md, so a cairn typed a name before the shelf landed under another one -- present at HEAD, two occurrences, one file, no peer in flight. Repointed both and the guard reads GREEN, RED and GREEN paths both proven. No new row: this is the class the card already books as *a number a hand types that a landing falsifies*, and the only thing it widens is that the class is not only numbers. The card's lantern line now reads four rather than three and names the shelf NAME as the fourth.
obs That repair cost the send a second card sweep, which is worth recording as cost rather than as narrative: the entry had already fitted 40,958 of 40,960, so a fourth line meant freeing more, and what gave way was the %424 block's closing sentence -- take the number from --next, never from the row -- which the card's own Standing block carries word for word two screens up. Card 40,939, 21 free.
obs A procedural red of my own, recorded because it cost real work: git stash push -- <paths> followed by git reset --hard discards every modification NOT in the stashed set. I stashed three files to rebase, reset, and lost three edits that were sitting unstashed in the working tree. They were scripted, so they reproduced exactly -- which is the only reason it cost minutes rather than the lap. Stash everything or stash nothing.
obs The dead lap's own log lands, with its erratum rewritten to say plainly what happened: its scan and control are the peer's now and theirs are better, and the record stands because the duplication IS the measurement. Two ships spent one lap each on one finding, an hour apart, with nothing in the ledger to say the red was being worked -- which is exactly the question the card has been carrying for six laps.
loom roster=cold guards_run=175 red=1 gated=3 passes=2 both_died_midrun=yes
loom control=compass_station cases=14 pass=14 fail=0 note=dropped_for_peers_18
loom replay=compass_station ref=dfcb4303eb station_hollow=2 verdict=hollow_station exit=1
loom rota=itinerary_compute swept=256 bad=0 verdict=ok
loom qa=construction/README.md elder_meter_before=77 elder_meter_after=85 shipped_meter_before=84 shipped_meter_after=84 setting=door service=75
loom qa=docs/PROVEN_SEAT.md grade=89 setting=field service=75
loom qa=context/rankings.kyri grade=93 setting=meter
loom card_bytes before=40954 freed=425 entries=2 after=40939 free=21 rebases=2 sweeps=2
file construction/README.md the seventh living surface -- six false claims, the health line, the fleet clause; B/84 before and after under the shipped meter, the repair being to its truth
file context/rankings.kyri the 27-slot rota's ranks 21 and 22 tended pointer stubs; both now read the card, and the room name work-in-progress carried to construction
file docs/PROVEN_SEAT.md the suite-nib pointer sent to the card, its elder room named
file construction/EQUINOX_SEAT_MAP.md the living-calendar line sent to the card
file construction/CHECKPOINTS.md two cairns -- the README molt, recovered and landed, and the card sweep that made room
file construction/ITINERARY.md the account, two condenses freeing 712, and the drop named plainly
file session-logs/date/20260906/20260906-062244_the-rose-that-pointed-at-two-signposts.kyri the dead lap's own log, recovered under %464 with an honest erratum
scope one dead lap read back and mostly dropped to a peer, one door molted, three more instances of a booked red repaired on touch
status GREEN -- readme_reach GREEN after the repoint with both paths proven, compass_rose GREEN on metal, compass_station_control 18/18 at HEAD, index_row_bound ok, itinerary_compute swept 256 bad=0, tracked_link ok, log_has_a_row GREEN, both pins under bound
recommend keep-going -- the prose product has room now that the rose is closed and its instances are swept; the next petrichor lap takes docs-geode work rather than widening this class further, since whether a page's claim about a live card earns a meter of its own is already one sentence of Keaton's on the card.
